# Hostile audit — Type-II linear loaded-unit-side floor

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_linear_non_tooth_boundary_deficit_2026-09-12.md`  
**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only scope, with a stronger retained defect tradeoff.**  
**Theorem authority:** unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The reviewed theorem combines the audited-working Type-II hull-side theorem with
canonical Section 63 and audited-working §142. The main conclusion is

\[
\liminf {e_{BB}\over D}
\ge {\gamma\over2},
\qquad
\gamma=2\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}-\frac\pi2.
\]

Because `c_B=0` in Type II, every edge counted by `e_BB` is an actual unit side of the
loaded force hull.

## 1. Exact algebra

For sufficiently large Type-II survivors, the independently audited hull-side theorem
sets `c_B=0` in the canonical Section-63 identity

\[
e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B+c_B.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B.}
\tag{A.1}
\]

Every bounded nonquadrilateral face has degree at least five, so

\[
Q\ge f.
\tag{A.2}
\]

Therefore one actually has the stronger finite inequality

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}
\ge f+2k+u_B
={2f-k\over2}+{5k\over2}+u_B.
}
\tag{A.3}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}-{5k\over2}-u_B
\ge {2f-k\over2}.
}
\tag{A.4}
\]

Audited-working §142 gives

\[
\liminf {2f-k\over D}\ge\gamma.
\]

Hence the strongest direct asymptotic consequence of this chain is

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}
\left(e_{BB}-{5k\over2}-u_B\right)
\ge {\gamma\over2}.
}
\tag{A.5}
\]

Dropping the nonnegative defect terms recovers the reviewed headline floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {e_{BB}\over D}\ge {\gamma\over2}
=0.166914643466509\ldots .
}
\tag{A.6}
\]

The reviewed proof used the equivalent upper chain
`2f-k<=2e_BB-5k-2u_B<=2e_BB`; its signs are correct.

**Verdict:** PASS, with (A.5) retained as the sharper form.

## 2. Conversion to hull geometry is valid only in Type II

The conclusion that all `e_BB` contacts are force-hull sides uses the separately
audited Type-II theorem `c_B=0`. Without that theorem, `e_BB` can include loaded-loaded
unit chords that are not hull sides. The reviewed note states this scope explicitly.

With `c_B=0`, if `m` is the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides, then

\[
e_{BB}=q-m.
\]

Audited-working §130 gives

\[
r_{deep}=m-T_{\triangle}\ge0.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{q-T_{\triangle}=e_{BB}+r_{deep}.}
\tag{A.7}
\]

Combining (A.6) and (A.7) proves the linear non-tooth deficit. No stopped-train count
is confused with the augmented triangular-face count.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. Constant check

With

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
\gamma=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over2},
\]

one has

\[
{\gamma\over2}
=\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}-{\pi\over4}
=0.166914643466509\ldots .
\]

No rounded decimal is used in the proof.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. Occurrence, bridge, and source-point scope

The §142 lower bound is built from the occurrence-counted stopped-train ledger and
explicitly retains singleton trains, bridges, and bounded exceptional faces. The
Section-63 quantities `Q,k,u_B` are global exact ledger terms. Hence no simple-boundary
substitution enters (A.3).

Contact-isolated source points are untouched. Neither Section 63 nor §142 inserts them
into the Section-57 core affine kernel.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Explicit non-implications

The theorem does not prove:

- a positive lower bound on KKT stress carried by the unit hull sides;
- a long consecutive run of unit hull sides;
- that a unit hull side belongs to an outer-to-outer train;
- that the linear unit-side set alone forces a unit equilateral triangle; or
- a same-cardinality diameter-decreasing deformation.

In particular, an oblique near-triangular bulk with a boundary-order defect layer is
not excluded merely by (A.6).

## Final disposition

**PASS at proof-only scope.** Future use should prefer the stronger defect-sensitive
form (A.5). The exact loaded-unit-side floor is a genuine new structural constraint,
but C-II remains open.
